        <body>
  <h2>Student Rights, Discipline, Manifestation Determination, & Due Process in District 27 (Rockaway)</h2>

  <p>In District 27 (Broad Channel, Howard Beach, Ozone Park, Rockaway, Woodhaven), ensuring a safe and supportive learning environment for all students is paramount. This requires clear disciplinary procedures that respect student rights, particularly for students with disabilities who are protected under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act.</p>

  <h3>Understanding Disciplinary Procedures in District 27</h3>

  <p>The New York City Department of Education (NYC DOE) has a citywide Discipline Code that outlines the rules of conduct for students and the range of disciplinary responses schools can employ. This code applies to all schools in District 27. However, for students with Individualized Education Programs (IEPs) or 504 Plans, additional protections are in place to prevent discriminatory discipline practices.</p>

  <p>It's crucial for parents to understand that disciplinary actions against students with disabilities must be carefully considered in light of their disability. This is where the Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) process becomes critical.</p>

  <h3>Manifestation Determination Review (MDR): A Deep Dive</h3>

  <p>A Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) is a legally mandated process triggered when a student with an IEP or 504 plan faces a suspension exceeding 10 school days or a change in placement due to a violation of school rules (34 CFR § 300.530).  The purpose of the MDR is to determine if the student's misconduct is a "manifestation" of their disability. In simpler terms, the team must decide if the behavior was caused by, or directly and substantially related to, the student's disability, or if it was a direct result of the school's failure to implement the IEP or 504 Plan correctly.</p>

  <p><b>Who is on the MDR Team?</b></p>
  <p>The MDR team must include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The school district representative (often a school administrator or special education administrator).</li>
    <li>The parent(s) or legal guardian(s) of the student.</li>
    <li>Relevant members of the student's IEP team or 504 plan team (e.g., special education teacher, general education teacher, school psychologist, related service providers).</li>
    <li>The student (when appropriate and possible).</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>The MDR Process: A Step-by-Step Guide for District 27 Parents</b></p>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Suspension Notice:</b> The school must provide written notice to the parents of the suspension and inform them of their right to participate in the MDR. This notice should clearly state the charges against the student and the length of the suspension.</li>
    <li><b>Record Review:</b> The MDR team will review all relevant information in the student's file. This includes:
        <ul>
            <li>The student's IEP or 504 Plan</li>
            <li>Evaluation data (psychological, educational, medical reports)</li>
            <li>Observations of the student's behavior</li>
            <li>Any relevant information provided by the parents</li>
            <li>Discipline records</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><b>MDR Meeting:</b> The team convenes to discuss the information gathered and answer two critical questions, as per IDEA regulations (34 CFR § 300.530(e)):
        <ul>
            <li>Was the conduct in question caused by, or did it have a direct and substantial relationship to, the child's disability?</li>
            <li>Was the conduct in question the direct result of the school district's failure to implement the IEP?</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><b>Determination:</b> The team must make a determination regarding each of these questions. The determination must be documented in writing.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><b>What Happens If the Behavior IS a Manifestation of the Disability?</b></p>
  <p>If the MDR team determines that the behavior *is* a manifestation of the student's disability, the school district has specific obligations:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA):</b> If an FBA has not already been conducted, the school must conduct one to identify the function of the student's behavior (i.e., what purpose does the behavior serve for the student?). (34 CFR § 300.530(f)(1)(i))</li>
    <li><b>Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP):</b>  The school must either develop a BIP, or review and revise an existing BIP, to address the behavior. The BIP should be based on the findings of the FBA and should include positive behavioral interventions, strategies, and supports. (34 CFR § 300.530(f)(1)(ii))</li>
    <li><b>Return to Placement:</b>  The student must be returned to their original placement unless the parents and the school district agree to a change of placement as part of the BIP development or revision.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>What Happens If the Behavior is NOT a Manifestation of the Disability?</b></p>
  <p>If the MDR team determines that the behavior is *not* a manifestation of the student's disability, the same disciplinary procedures that apply to students without disabilities may be applied to the student with a disability (34 CFR § 300.530(c)). However, the school district must still provide services to the student during the period of suspension to enable them to continue to participate in the general education curriculum, although in another setting, and to progress toward meeting the goals set out in the student's IEP (34 CFR § 300.530(d)).</p>

  <h3>Parent Advocacy Tips for MDRs in District 27</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Prepare Thoroughly:</b> Before the MDR meeting, gather all relevant information about your child's disability, IEP/504 plan, and the circumstances surrounding the disciplinary incident.  Document everything.</li>
        <li><b>Bring an Advocate:</b>  Consider bringing a special education advocate or attorney to the MDR meeting. They can provide support, ensure your child's rights are protected, and help you navigate the complex legal and procedural requirements.</li>
        <li><b>Actively Participate:</b>  Share your insights and concerns during the MDR meeting. Don't hesitate to ask questions and challenge assumptions made by the school staff.</li>
        <li><b>Document the Meeting:</b>  Take detailed notes during the MDR meeting, or ask a friend or advocate to do so. This can be helpful if you need to appeal the MDR determination.</li>
        <li><b>Request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE):</b> If you disagree with the school's evaluation of your child, you have the right to request an IEE at public expense (under certain circumstances). This can provide valuable information for the MDR process.</li>
        <li><b>Be Aware of Timelines:</b> The MDR must be conducted within 10 school days of the decision to change the student's placement due to a disciplinary infraction. (34 CFR § 300.530(e)). Make sure the school adheres to this timeline.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Common District 27 Pitfalls in MDRs and How to Avoid Them</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Insufficient IEP Implementation:</b> The MDR team may fail to adequately consider whether the student's behavior was a result of the school's failure to implement the IEP.  Parents should be prepared to provide specific examples of how the IEP was not followed.  Document all instances of non-compliance.</li>
        <li><b>Predetermined Outcomes:</b>  Sometimes, MDR outcomes appear predetermined.  If you suspect this is the case, document your concerns and consider filing a complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED).</li>
        <li><b>Lack of Parent Involvement:</b> The school may not adequately involve parents in the MDR process. Insist on being an equal participant and having your voice heard.</li>
        <li><b>Inadequate FBAs/BIPs:</b>  The FBA may be poorly conducted, or the BIP may not be appropriate or effective.  If this is the case, challenge the FBA/BIP and demand revisions.  Consider requesting an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) focused on behavior.</li>
    </ul>

  <h3>Due Process Rights</h3>

  <p>If you disagree with the outcome of the MDR or any other aspect of your child's special education, you have the right to file a due process complaint with the NYSED (34 CFR § 300.507). Filing a due process complaint initiates a formal legal process that can result in a hearing before an impartial hearing officer (IHO). You have two years from the date of the action that you are complaining about to file a due process complaint.  The IHO can order the school district to take corrective action, such as providing compensatory education, revising the IEP, or changing the student's placement.</p>

  <p><b>What Issues Can Be Addressed in Due Process?</b></p>
  <p>You can file a due process complaint regarding any matter relating to the identification, evaluation, or educational placement of your child, or the provision of a free appropriate public education (FAPE) to your child. Common issues that are raised in due process complaints include:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Failure to provide appropriate services</li>
        <li>Inadequate IEP development</li>
        <li>Inappropriate placement</li>
        <li>Disciplinary actions</li>
        <li>Failure to conduct timely evaluations</li>
    </ul>

  <h3>Advice Regarding Suspensions in District 27</h3>

  <p>If your child in District 27 faces suspension, remember these key points:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Know Your Rights:</b> Familiarize yourself with the NYC Department of Education's citywide Discipline Code and the IDEA regulations regarding disciplinary procedures for students with disabilities. Access the Discipline Code on the NYC DOE website.</li>
    <li><b>Request Information:</b> Obtain detailed written documentation about the charges leading to the suspension, including specific dates, times, locations, witnesses, and evidence. Demand to see any video footage or other evidence.</li>
    <li><b>Attend Hearings:</b> Actively participate in any hearings or meetings related to the suspension. Bring an advocate or attorney to represent your child's interests. Be prepared to present your side of the story and challenge the school's allegations.</li>
    <li><b>Advocate:</b> If your child has an IEP or 504 plan, immediately request an MDR if the suspension exceeds 10 days or involves a change of placement. Ensure the MDR is conducted properly and that your voice is heard.</li>
    <li><b>Seek Support:</b>  Consider contacting a legal aid organization or advocacy group specializing in student rights if you need assistance.  Organizations like Advocates for Children of New York (AFC) and the Legal Aid Society offer free or low-cost legal services.
    <li><b>Document Everything:</b> Keep detailed records of all communications with the school, including emails, letters, and phone calls. This documentation can be invaluable if you need to file a complaint or pursue legal action.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Understanding your child's rights and actively participating in the disciplinary process is essential to ensuring a fair outcome. Never hesitate to seek outside support from advocates or attorneys who specialize in special education law.</p>

  <h3>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)</h3>

  <dl>
    <dt><b>Q: What is the difference between a suspension and a removal?</b></dt>
    <dd>A: A suspension is a disciplinary action that removes a student from the classroom for a specific period. A removal can refer to a change of placement, which may be temporary or permanent. Removals that exceed 10 school days trigger the MDR process for students with IEPs or 504 Plans.</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: My child was suspended for 5 days. Do I still have any rights?</b></dt>
    <dd>A: Yes. Even for suspensions of less than 10 days, students with IEPs or 504 Plans have the right to have their disability considered. While a full MDR may not be required, the school must still ensure that the suspension is not discriminatory and that the student continues to receive appropriate educational services.</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: The school is refusing to conduct an FBA. What can I do?</b></dt>
    <dd>A: If you believe your child needs an FBA and the school is refusing to conduct one, you have several options:
        <ul>
            <li>Request an IEP meeting to discuss your concerns.</li>
            <li>File a complaint with the NYSED.</li>
            <li>File a due process complaint.</li>
        </ul></dd>

    <dt><b>Q: Can the school expel my child with an IEP?</b></dt>
    <dd>A: Expulsion is a severe disciplinary action that permanently removes a student from school. While it is possible for a student with an IEP to be expelled, the process is subject to strict legal requirements, including the MDR. The school must demonstrate that the expulsion is not related to the student's disability and that all due process rights have been followed.</dd>
    
     <dt><b>Q: What are "interim alternative educational settings (IAES)"?</b></dt>
    <dd>A: An IAES is a temporary placement for a student who has been suspended or removed from their regular classroom for disciplinary reasons. The IAES must allow the student to continue to participate in the general education curriculum, although in another setting, and to continue to receive the services and supports outlined in their IEP. Federal law defines the IAES requirements and duration depending on the circumstances. (34 CFR § 300.530(g))</dd>
  </dl>
</body>
